  vehje@origin/main                              248

  what the suppressed lines actually carry

    667  carry a host primitive at a position the exception does not
         cover. These are the demand, stated by the consumer.
     43  sit on a line that is nothing but a comment, and are inert. The
         pack's `line_lint_allowed` reads one line and no neighbour, and
         `no_bare_numeric` skips a line whose text begins `//` before it
         scans, so a marker written above the item it means to cover
         suppresses nothing and the line below it is still checked.
     71  carry nothing but the type of a const generic parameter, which op
         excepted by name and which the pack already blanks before it scans.
    643  sit on a line the pack's own scan would not have flagged either,
         so they suppress a diagnostic that was never going to fire. The
         pack's rule is reimplemented in `allows::the_pack_would_flag`
         rather than approximated, because the question is about that
         instrument and not about this one.

  the inert ones are filed under: #72 x18, #121 x17, #345 x5, #654 x2, #644 x1

  the excepted ones are filed under: #121 x70, #649 x1

the supply, at the same bar
  the same walk over the same positions, keeping the stack's own type names
  instead of the host's. The list is hand-written and is printed below, so a
  name missing from it undercounts this side and makes the obligation look
  further from met than it is.

  arvo               142
  gone               954
  notko              200

  193 host, 954 naming an arvo crate or type that is not on
  arvo's `dev`, and 342 resolving. So 12% of the positions carrying
  any of the three still name the host, and a further 64%
  name arvo and reach nothing. Counting the second group as supply, which
  one undivided arvo list does, is what makes the obligation look nearly
  met when half of what meets it does not exist.

